   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the correction law, in relation to providing records for\nprotection and advocacy services in a timely manner",
    "basePrintNo": "S5511",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5511-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §626, Cor L",
    "lawSection": "Correction Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5511\n\nSPONSOR: SEPULVEDA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the correction law, in relation to\nproviding records for protection and advocacy services in a timely\nmanner\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo provide records to the organization appointed by the governor to act\nas the federally-mandated protection and advocacy organization repres-\nenting disabled New Yorkers.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the correction law by adding a new section 626 to\ninstruct DOCCS to provide records to the protection and advocacy organ-\nization within three days of the receipt of a records request.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nDisability Rights New York (DRNY) is a state-wide non-profit organiza-\ntion appointed by the governor to provide individual representation and\nsystemic advocacy on behalf of individuals with disabilities under the\nfederal Protection and Advocacy (P&A) law. To accomplish this work,\nCongress has granted P0 broad access authority, including access to\nrecords held by agencies who provide services to individuals with disa-\nbilities. State prisons and jails fall under P&A jurisdiction.\n\nDRNY regularly makes written requests to DOCCS for records pursuant to\nP&A authority. Federal law requires access to records be provided either\nwithin 24 hours, three business days, or \"promptly,\" depending on the\nnature of the request. DOCCS' practice is to only issue copies of\nrecords after P&A staff have reviewed records at the prison where the\ninmate is located, calculated the page count, and paid fees for the\nrecords. The records are located at prisons throughout the state, and\nmost records follow inmates when they transfer to another prison; there-\nfore access at the facility is frequently not feasible or is an ineffi-\ncient option.Even after receipt of payment, DOCCS generally takes weeks\nor months to provide DRNY with the requested records.\n\nIt is critically important to the work of our state's protection and\nadvocacy system and the people with disabilities they serve that DRNY\ntimely receive records. This bill requires DOCCS to produce records in a\ntimely fashion upon receipt of written request. New York must comply\nwith federal law. Since DRNY is appointed by the governor to provide\nthese services, DOCCS should provide the same treatment afforded to\nstate agencies, to whom they provide records free of charge upon\nrequest.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNegligible\n \nLOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately\n",
